course: dictionary
lesson: 6872
title: Overhear: hearing without meaning to
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To overhear something is to hear what other people are saying when they do not know you can hear, or when you did not mean to listen. The word is built from «over», which means across or past, and «hear». Stories often start this way: a character overhears a secret plan and the adventure begins.
explain: Read the clues around the word. «Then he overheard his aunties talking.» Ask: did he mean to listen? The sentence does not say he hid or crept closer, so he heard them by chance. Try the swap: «Then he heard his aunties talking» still works, so overheard means heard, but by accident.
explain: Overhear is a verb, so its form changes with time: overhears, overheard, overhearing. Use overheard for the past. You can say «I couldn't help overhearing», which means you could not stop yourself hearing it. Be careful with private talk: if you overhear something that is not yours to share, keep it to yourself and be kind.
fact: To overhear means to hear something by accident, without the speaker knowing you can hear.
fact: «Overheard» is the past tense of overhear.
fact: Listening on purpose, such as creeping close to a door to hear a secret, is called eavesdropping.
fact: «I couldn't help overhearing» means you heard something without trying to.
fact: Some things you overhear are private, so be kind and do not repeat them.
exercise: «Then he overheard his aunties talking.» What does overheard mean here? | He stopped his aunties from talking | He sang a song to his aunties | He asked his aunties to talk louder | *He heard his aunties talking without them knowing
exercise: «And when Mia's mum overheard Mia's friends swearing, Mia asked them to keep it clean.» Who did the overhearing? | Mia | Mia's friends | Nobody | *Mia's mum
exercise: Yesterday I ___ my brother planning a surprise party. Which word fits? | *overheard | overhear | overhears | overhearing
test: «I couldn't help overhearing» means | I asked them to whisper | I was too busy to listen | *I could not stop myself hearing what was said | I shouted over them so they stopped
test: Which one is eavesdropping, not overhearing? | *Creeping to the door to listen in on a secret talk | Hearing your name called across the playground | Hearing a neighbour's radio through the wall | Hearing rain on the roof while you read
exam: The Rat, chewing a straw, asks, «did I overhear you say something about "we," and "start," and "this afternoon?"» What does the Rat mean? | The Rat is asking for a drink | The Rat is writing a letter | *The Rat may have heard a plan by accident and wants to know more | The Rat has gone home for lunch
